  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef ml_imputation(matrix: list, strategy: str = 'mean') -> list:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Missing Value Imputation** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef ml_imputation(matrix: list, strategy: str = 'mean') -> list:\n    arr = np.array(matrix, dtype=np.float64)\n    out = arr.copy()\n    for c in range(arr.shape[1]):\n        valid = arr[:, c][~np.isnan(arr[:, c])]\n        fill = np.median(valid) if strategy == 'median' else (np.mean(valid) if len(valid) else 0.0)\n        out[np.isnan(out[:, c]), c] = fill\n    return np.round(out, 6).tolist()\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
